{% extends 'demos/upload/demo.html.twig' %}

{% block demo_container %}
    <div class="UploadDemoShell max-w-[1140px] mx-auto mb-12">
        <twig:Browser id="demo" class="shadow-blur shadow-blur--rainbow w-full" frameClass="">
            <div class="UploadStage LiveAvatar">
                <div class="UploadStage_container">
                    <twig:UploadAvatar />
                    <button
                        type="button"
                        class="UploadStage_sample"
                        data-controller="upload-samples"
                        data-upload-samples-kind-value="images"
                        data-upload-samples-names-value="{{ ['profile-pic-final-v2.jpg']|json_encode }}"
                        data-upload-samples-for-value=".LiveAvatar input[type=file]"
                        data-action="upload-samples#fill"
                    >Use a sample photo</button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </twig:Browser>
    </div>

    <section class="UploadDemoStory">
        <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto px-6 md:px-12">
            <twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow
                filename="src/Twig/Components/UploadAvatar.php"
                :lineStart="24"
                :lineEnd="49"
                reversed
                sticky
            >
## A prop that accepts an upload

Only a nullable string prop carrying `#[UploadTarget]` can receive an upload:
the attribute is the allow-list. The prop stores the same signed reference a
form field sends.

`getUpload('photo')` resolves it into an `UploadedFile`, or `null`.
            </twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow>

            <twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow
                filename="templates/components/UploadAvatar.html.twig"
                sticky
            >
## The bridge applies it

The `live-upload` controller wraps the field. When the upload completes, it
reads the signed reference from the field event and calls the `applyUpload`
live action.

The component renders again with the name and the size it received: no form
submission, no page reload. `data-live-ignore` keeps the field, and the picture
the browser drew, out of that render.

The other way round, the `remove` event of the field calls `clearUpload`: taking
the file out empties the prop.
            </twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow>

            <twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow
                filename="src/Form/AvatarUploadForm.php"
                :lineStart="26"
                :lineEnd="32"
                reversed
                sticky
            >
## The picture stays in the browser

The field is a plain `UploadType`. `show_preview` draws the picture from the
file the visitor selected. The avatar is that preview, enlarged in CSS: this
public server keeps no file and sends none back.

To keep the photo in your application, resolve the prop in your own live action
and pass the upload to the service that stores it.
            </twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
